  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "The number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;digit numbers, that can be formed using all the digits of the number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>123412341</mn></math> so that the even digits occupy only even places, is ______",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "60",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>According to the question,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p>there are four even&nbsp;digits as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> are repeating two times each.&nbsp;<br />So,&nbsp;the even digits will occupy the even places in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn></math> ways</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> are repeating two and three times&nbsp;respectively.<br />So, the remaining places will be occupied by odd&nbsp;digits in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math> ways</p><p>Therefore, total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;digits that can be formed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>60</mn></math>.<br />Hence, the answer is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
